# login.term: account session
#
# THIS IS THE ONE LOGIN ACTUALLY ASKS FOR. Tracing the opens shows it requesting login.term and
# never login, so a policy installed only under the shorter name changes nothing and the fallback
# to /etc/pam.d/other, which denies everything, is what answers.
#
# See the login policy beside this file for why Cider does not use Apple's: theirs is built on
# pam_opendirectory, and there is no directory service in this container.
account    required       pam_permit.so
# NO pam_launchd.so: openpam cannot load it and pam_start then fails for the whole policy,
# whatever the control flag says.
session    required       pam_permit.so
